      "lead_quote": "For twenty years, I've sat in this chair / And I've seen the other side",
      "lyrics": "[Verse 1]\nThis red suit, it's a funny thing\nIt's just some fleece and polyester\nBut people see what they wanna see\nA magic man, a kindly jester\nThey don't see the guy inside it\nWho's just punchin' a holiday clock\nWho's countin' down the minutes\nOn this cardboard, tinsel rock\n[Verse 2]\nFor twenty years, I've sat in this chair\nAnd I've seen the other side\nOf the \"perfect, happy Christmas\"\nAll the places people hide\nI've seen the parents fightin'\nIn a whisper, 'bout the cost\nI've held the kids who just want daddy\nTo come home from bein' lost\n[Chorus]\nI'm not Santa, son, not really\nI'm just a man named Joe\nWho's seen a lot of sadness\nWatchin' all these seasons come and go\nYou learn to spot the real cold\nNot the kind that's in the air\nBut the kind that's in the eyes\nOf a soul that's stripped down bare\n[Verse 3]\nSo, yeah, I saw you by the entrance\nLookin' hollow, lookin' through\nYou were a mirror of a feelin'\nThat I've known a time or two\nAnd I'm not rich, I'm not a savior\nI just get paid to \"ho ho ho\"\nBut a man deserves a hamburger\nWhen the wind begins to blow\n[Chorus]\nI'm not Santa, son, not really\nI'm just a man named Joe\nWho's seen a lot of sadness\nWatchin' all these seasons come and go\nYou learn to spot the real cold\nNot the kind that's in the air\nBut the kind that's in the eyes\nOf a soul that's stripped down bare\n[Outro]\nThis red suit... it's just a job.\nI'm not the real Santa...\nI'm just Joe.",
      "story": "This contemplative folk song shifts the narrative perspective to Joe, the \"store Santa.\" In a gentle, weary baritone, Joe explains why he helped William. He's not a saint; he's just a man named Joe, clocking in at a seasonal job. He's spent years watching the \"shady\" side of Christmas from his plastic throne—seeing the parental arguments, the disappointment, and the deep, real-world sadness that exists just behind the festive smiles. He helps William not out of pity, but out of a shared sense of humanity and a recognition of the same bone-deep cold he has felt in his own life.\n\nNarratively, this movement represents (Contemplative Folk) The mentor's song. Joe tells his own story. He's not a saint; he's just a man who's seen \"sadness\" and loss. He explains why he helps, humanizing himself.. Develops the Mentor. This builds the \"social connection\". It's not a savior, but a \"peer\" in the human experience, solidifying the bond from Track 8.",
