      "lead_quote": "There's a hissing from the radiator / A single bulb hangs from the wire",
      "lyrics": "[Verse 1]\nThere's a hissing from the radiator\nA single bulb hangs from the wire\nThere's a crack across the ceiling\nAnd the window sticks a little higher\nBut the wind ain't cutting through me\nThe concrete ain't my bed\nAnd the fear I knew... it's quiet now\nThere's just a pillow for my head\n[Chorus]\nAnd it's a different kind of warmth\nIt's not the poison, not the lie\nIt's the sound of a key in a keyhole\nIt's a roof that's old and dry\nI'm still poor, I'm not \"cured\"\nI'm just a man who's payin' rent\nFor a little square of safety\nAnd my first sober winter...\nIs the best one I've ever spent\n[Verse 2]\nI still wake up, shakin' sometimes\nThinkin' I'm still on the grate\nI reach my hand out in the darkness\nAnd I feel the wall, and I just wait\nAnd the panic, it subsides\nWhen I remember where I am\nIn a room that's mine, in a life I'm buildin'\nA broken, stubborn, lucky man\n[Chorus]\nYeah, it's a different kind of warmth\nIt's not the poison, not the lie\nIt's the sound of a key in a keyhole\nIt's a roof that's old and dry\nI'm still poor, I'm not \"cured\"\nI'm just a man who's payin' rent\nFor a little square of safety\nAnd my first sober winter...\nIs the best one I've ever spent\n[Bridge]\nI'm over the moon...\nJust to be in a simple room\nJust to watch the snow fall on the glass\nFrom this side\nFrom this side... at last\n[Chorus]\nYeah, it's a different kind of warmth\nIt's not the poison, not the lie\nIt's the sound of a key in a keyhole\nIt's a roof that's old and dry\nI'm still poor, I'm not \"cured\"\nI'm just a man who's payin' rent\nFor a little square of safety\nAnd my first sober winter...\n...is the best one I've ever spent\n[Outro]\nMy first sober winter...",
      "story": "This is the album's fragile \"redemption\" song, the narrative equivalent of Southeastern's \"Cover Me Up.\" In a profoundly intimate, acoustic folk style, William sings from a simple, rented room. He's not \"cured,\" he's not rich, but he is inside. The song is a quiet, awestruck reflection on the small, sacred details: a radiator that hisses, a window that locks, and the profound, almost overwhelming relief of not being cold. It's not about joy; it's about the \"different kind of warmth\" that comes from safety and the hard-won, fragile peace of his first sober winter.\n\nNarratively, this movement represents (Intimate Folk) William's perspective from a simple, rented room. It's his \"first sober winter.\" It's still cold, he's still poor, but he's inside. He's not \"cured,\" but he's \"over the moon\".. The Southeastern Moment. This is our \"Cover Me Up\". A profound, personal, and fragile redemption. The goal of Track 10 is achieved: he is safe.",
