      "lead_quote": "Oh, Joe... you're my unlikely friend / You bought a hamburger for a stranger",
      "lyrics": "[Verse 1]\nOne of us was a ghost in the snow\nOne of us was just punchin' a clock\nOne of us was just waitin' to die\nOne of us was just takin' a walk\nOne of us saw a problem, a stain\nOne of us saw a mirror, a man\nIt's the most unlikely story\nThat I'll ever understand\n[Chorus]\nOh, Joe... you're my unlikely friend\nYou bought a hamburger for a stranger\nAnd brought a story to an end\nAnd started a new one\nYou were the ladder, you were the grace\nWho saw a human being\nIn a cold and broken place\nYou're my unlikely friend\n[Verse 2]\nYou didn't preach, you didn't judge\nYou just sat down in the booth\nYou didn't see a hopeless junkie\nYou just saw the simple truth\nThat a man was cold, and a man was lost\nAnd a man just needed a hand\nYou did the one thing no one else would\nYou decided to understand\n[Chorus]\nOh, Joe... you're my unlikely friend\nYou bought a hamburger for a stranger\nAnd brought a story to an end\nAnd started a new one\nYou were the ladder, you were the grace\nWho saw a human being\nIn a cold and broken place\nYou're my unlikely friend\n[Bridge]\nYou were the forty-four percent\nYou were the seventy-six\nYou were the one who fought the system\nWhen it was all just broken sticks\nYou showed me how to fight back\nYou showed me how to be\nThe man I thought was buried\nThat I could never set free\n[Chorus]\nOh, Joe... you're my unlikely friend!\nYou bought a hamburger for a stranger\nAnd brought a story to an end\nAnd started a new one!\nYou were the ladder, you were the grace\nWho saw a human being\nIn a cold and broken place!\nYou're my unlikely friend!\n[Outro]\nMy unlikely friend\nThank you, Joe",
      "story": "This is the album's emotional climax, an uplifting Americana anthem of pure gratitude. Building from the fragile quiet of the previous track, this song is a full-throated \"thank you\" from William to Joe. It celebrates their \"unlikely friendship\" and the radical, life-changing power of that first encounter. William traces the line from being a \"ghost in the snow\" to a man with a key, all because a \"store Santa\" chose to see him. It's a cathartic release, a celebration of the man who was his \"44-point friend.\"\n\nNarratively, this movement represents (Uplifting Americana) A song of gratitude for Joe, inspired by the narrative of \"Eddie\". It's about \"unlikely friendships\" and the \"shared affiliation\" that changed everything.. Emotional Climax. Provides the cathartic release, celebrating the album's central, positive theme, building on the profound gratitude of Track 12.",
